1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,891
Two months into the year. Is your plan working?

2
00:00:03,311 --> 00:00:08,411
Let's check our example from episodes one and eighteen, then you do yours, in four questions.

3
00:00:09,181 --> 00:00:10,134
Four questions.

4
00:00:10,904 --> 00:00:13,120
Question one. Is the debt going down?

5
00:00:13,540 --> 00:00:23,703
On the real plan from episode 18 ($373 a month), the card went from $3,200 to $2,576. Down $624 in 60 days.

6
00:00:24,123 --> 00:00:31,435
About $122 of the payments went to interest. That's the cost of carrying the balance, and why every extra dollar matters.

7
00:00:32,205 --> 00:00:34,531
Question two. Did the surprise fund work?

8
00:00:34,951 --> 00:00:48,382
Two paychecks at $66, then two at $76 after the month-one fix: about $284 saved. In month one, the oil change broke the budget. Next time, it comes out of the fund. That's the system working.

9
00:00:49,152 --> 00:00:51,424
Question three. Is the plan still true?

10
00:00:51,844 --> 00:00:59,526
Put planned next to actual again. If a category keeps missing, change the number, not your willpower. Episode eighteen shows how.

11
00:01:00,296 --> 00:01:02,508
Question four. What's the next big move?

12
00:01:02,928 --> 00:01:08,612
For our example, it's the tax refund. Put $1,800 on the card in March, and look what happens.

13
00:01:09,032 --> 00:01:13,430
Debt-free in month 5 instead of 10, and about $163 less interest.

14
00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:15,132
So, your four questions.

15
00:01:15,292 --> 00:01:17,534
Debt going down? Surprise fund working?

16
00:01:17,694 --> 00:01:20,177
Plan still true? And what's the next big move?

17
00:01:20,597 --> 00:01:26,895
If the answers are mostly yes, you're doing better than most people who started a budget in January. Keep going.

18
00:01:27,665 --> 00:01:31,737
The Paycheck Planner in the description makes the check-in a five-minute job.

19
00:01:32,157 --> 00:01:34,388
Subscribe, and see you at the next check-in.
